\use{document}
\use{railroad}

\title{Railroad Diagrams from EBNF}
\author{the \mono{railroad} package}
\maketitle

The \mono{railroad} package turns a grammar written in W3C-style EBNF — the notation of the
XML and XPath specifications — into a railroad (syntax) diagram, one picture per rule. The whole
package is written in the document language over the \mono{\\picture} vector layer: there is no
railroad-specific engine primitive. This demo walks through every EBNF construct the package
draws, each shown in isolation, and closes with a complete realistic grammar.

A diagram has an entry arrow on the left, an exit arrow on the right, and the \italic{rail} running
between them through every item. The rule name sits in a band across the top of its own picture.

\section{Reading a rule}

A grammar is one or more rules separated by whitespace or newlines. Each rule names an expression
with \mono{::=}. The simplest rule is a single item — here a reference to another rule, drawn as
a plain rectangle (a \italic{nonterminal}).

\railroad{
  start ::= program
}

\section{The three kinds of item}

A \italic{primary} — the atom of a diagram — is one of three things, and each gets its own box
shape so you can tell them apart at a glance.

\subsection{Nonterminals}

A bare name is a \italic{nonterminal}: a reference to another rule. It is drawn as a square-cornered
rectangle.

\railroad{
  item ::= statement
}

\subsection{Terminals}

A quoted literal — with either double or single quotes — is a \italic{terminal}: text that appears
verbatim in the input. It is drawn as a rounded ``stadium'' box. The quotes are stripped; only the
contents are shown.

\railroad{
  keyword ::= "return"
}

A terminal may contain spaces; the lexer rejoins words across an open quote, so a multi-word literal
stays a single box.

\railroad{
  marker ::= "end of file"
}

\subsection{Character classes}

A bracketed character class is drawn as a pointed hexagon, with its brackets kept so the class
notation reads literally. Negated and ranged classes work the same way.

\railroad{
  digit ::= [0-9]
}

\railroad{
  notquote ::= [^"]
}

\section{Putting items in a row}

Writing two or more items in a row is \italic{concatenation}: they are drawn left to right, joined by
a connecting rail. This is a \italic{sequence}.

\railroad{
  call ::= name "(" args ")"
}

The difference operator \mono{-} of W3C EBNF is treated as plain concatenation, so \mono{A - B}
simply draws \mono{A} then \mono{B}.

\railroad{
  range ::= low - high
}

\section{Choosing between items}

The bar \mono{|} is \italic{alternation}: the branches are stacked and the rail forks to pick one.
The first alternative stays on the main line; later ones drop to a rounded fork below.

\railroad{
  sign ::= "+" | "-"
}

A choice may have any number of branches, and each branch may itself be a row of items.

\railroad{
  literal ::= number | "true" | "false" | string
}

\section{Grouping}

Parentheses group a sub-expression so a repetition or a choice can apply to the whole group rather
than to one item. A group is transparent — it adds no box of its own, only the structure inside it.
Here the choice \mono{("." \italic{or} ",")} is grouped so the row reads as a single column.

\railroad{
  pair ::= key (":" | "=") value
}

\section{Repetition marks}

A repetition mark after an item (or a group) controls how many times it may occur. Each mark draws a
distinct loop of rails.

\subsection{Optional --- \mono{?}}

A trailing \mono{?} makes an item \italic{optional}: the rail may skip it entirely, so the skip
runs straight along the main line and the item rides a forked track just below it.

\railroad{
  decl ::= "var" name "="? value
}

\subsection{Zero or more --- \mono{*}}

A trailing \mono{*} means \italic{zero or more}: a bypass above (to skip it) and a return loop below
(to repeat it).

\railroad{
  block ::= "{" statement* "}"
}

\subsection{One or more --- \mono{+}}

A trailing \mono{+} means \italic{one or more}: a return loop below to repeat, but no bypass, so at
least one occurrence is required.

\railroad{
  digits ::= digit+
}

\subsection{Repeating a group}

Because a group is just structure, a repetition mark applies to whatever it wraps — here a whole
parenthesised sequence repeats zero or more times.

\railroad{
  chain ::= head ("." method "(" ")")*
}

\section{Two diagrams the package draws cleverly}

The package recognises two idioms and lays them out the bottlecaps way — clearer than the literal
rails the EBNF would otherwise produce.

\subsection{Optional choice}

When an optional wraps a choice, \mono{(a | b)?}, the skip is folded \italic{into} the fork as an
empty top branch on the main line, with the real alternatives below it — one fork rather than a
choice nested inside a separate optional. A plain optional draws the same way, so the two match.

\railroad{
  visibility ::= ("public" | "private" | "protected")?
}

\subsection{Separated list}

The pattern \mono{A (sep A)*} — an item repeated with a separator between occurrences — is folded
into a single ``list'' node: the item \mono{A} rides the main line and the separator rides a
return loop above it. This is exactly how a comma-separated argument list should look.

\railroad{
  args ::= expr ("," expr)*
}

\section{A complete grammar}

A single \mono{\\railroad} call may hold several rules; they are drawn one after another. Here is a
small but realistic grammar — a JSON-ish value language — exercising every construct at once:
nonterminals, terminals, character classes, sequence, choice, grouping, all three repetition marks,
the optional-choice fold, and the separated-list fold.

\railroad{
  value   ::= object | array | string | number | "true" | "false" | "null"
  object  ::= "{" (member ("," member)*)? "}"
  member  ::= string ":" value
  array   ::= "[" (value ("," value)*)? "]"
}

The structural rules above use grouping, the optional-choice fold, and the separated-list fold
freely. The lexical rules below build a JSON number from character classes and repetition.

\railroad{
  number  ::= "-"? int frac? exp?
  int     ::= digit+
  frac    ::= "." digit+
  exp     ::= ("e" | "E") ("+" | "-")? digit+
  digit   ::= [0-9]
}

\section{Restyling the diagrams}

Every visual is a configuration variable you may override with
\mono{\\set name \{value\}} after \mono{\\use\{railroad\}}. Sizes are in points. Here we shrink the
boxes, widen the gaps, and recolour the palette to a warm hue — the terminal and character-class
shades are derived automatically as darker Oklch tints of the one base colour.

\set rrbase {#ffe0c0}
\set rrboxh {16}
\set rrfont {8}
\set rrhgap {20}
\set rrlinecolor {#5a3210}

\railroad{
  loop ::= "for" "(" init? ";" cond? ";" step? ")" body
}

The same grammar, the same package — only the knobs changed.
